Duplicate NetHack adds the option of rigging NetHack's random number 
generator so that two games can will have the same dungeon, items, 
monsters, and events.

To use it, apply the patch, compile and run NetHack with the -S option
  nethack -S somedungeon
Where 'somedungeon' is an arbitrary string which is used as a seed. 
Anyone who uses the same seed with the same version of NetHack will 
play a duplicate of your game.


How much will be the same?

- All dungeon levels (except the quest, if not playing the same 
  class) will have the same layout, trap locations, and items on the 
  floor. (Monsters may vary because they depend on player level).

- Monsters will be the same subject to level, meaning that if the 
  first randomly generated 9th-level creature player A sees is a mind 
  flayer, then the first 9th-level creature player B sees will be 
  a mind flayer too. (They could be in completely different places, 
  though; and player B might never see a 9th level monster at all.)

- Kicking sinks, drinking from fountains, and the like will produce 
  the same results, even if not from the same fountain. (Different 
  luck scores could change the result, though.)

- Item descriptions, the castle tune, and the result of asking for a 
  random class/race/alignment will not  be the same. These are 
  specific exceptions. (This means that player A might get a wish 
  from a smoky potion while player B doesn't, and that player A might 
  have a ring he can engrave with while player B doesn't.)

- Neither player will find or leave bones. It's the only way to be 
  fair.


How does it work?

This patch gives players choose the random number generator seed, so 
they can take the same sequence of random numbers. It also splits up 
the random number generator so that a number which gets used as an 
attack roll in game A won't get used as a random item in game B, and so 
on. In effect, it creates one RNG for attack rolls, another for item 
types, and so on.

Actually, it creates a separate random number generator for every place 
where dice are rolled. (There are about 2000 such places in NetHack. 
Also, when building a level, each dungeon level has its own set).

When the random number generator is called, a macro takes the file and 
line number of the call, the dungeon branch/level (if currently making 
a level), and possibly some other information, and hashes it toghether 
with MD5. Each hash value is a random number generator, which is 
created as-needed with an initial state that depends on the hash and on 
the seed.


Caveats

- Applying the patch breaks saved game compatibility.
- If you change any of the files in the src/ directory, your game may 
  not be comparable to others using the same seed.
- CPU usage, memory usage, and saved game size are all higher.


Suggestions

The main use of this is to play the same game as other people are 
playing, so that after you die you can compare scores and trade 
notes. Try playing game "nethack -S rgrn" and posting how it went.


Future Development

It would be nice if the game would automatically assign seeds, in such 
a way that you would never get the same seed twice but you would 
usually get a seed which other people have already used. Then, the 
scoreboard could show how you did compared to other people who played 
the same dungeon.

When random-type traps are placed in a new level, their type should not 
be decided immediately. Instead, it should be decided when the player 
first detects or triggers it. That way, if player A fell through a trap 
door, player B probably will too.

There's lots of room to speed up and improve the distribution of the 
random numbers produced. In fact, the present algorithm is probably the 
worst one that could possibly work.

Genociding or extincting a monster will cause players to see different 
monsters within that monster level, even if the genocided/extinct 
monster wouldn't normally appear. The ideal behaviour would be to 
generate the same sequence in both cases, but skip invalid monsters 
within that sequence.

Milky potions and smoky potions shouldn't be randomized, since that 
could lead to one player getting an extra wish or a nasty surprise. 
Rings should be shuffled, but which rings can engrave shouldn't.

Item generation should be handled more cleverly on the quest, to 
prevent the case where one player gets something nice (like a wand of 
wishing) and the other doesn't.


